; The ground: one modeled driveway standing at the origin, where three scaled
; boxes and a wood of rolled primitives used to be. Everything about it —
; what the model is, why it is a scene of its own, the quarter it is scaled by
; and why nothing out here has a collider — is in src/world/ground.gd and
; src/world/ground.tscn, beside the glb the numbers are measurements of.
;
; Nothing is overridden here, and that is the point of the split: the room's own
; scene file is back to holding one node called Ground, standing on the origin,
; the way it held three boxes standing on theirs. What the room asks of it is two
; questions — where the top of the drive is, so a car of any height can be sat on
; it, and how far the ground reaches, which is the fence the camera and
; everything held in front of it stay inside.
;
; A unique name because garage.gd holds it the way it holds %Car and %Camera:
; the drive's height is measured off it every time a car is parked, which is on
; the way in and again on every restyle.
[node name="Ground" parent="View/World" instance=ExtResource("6_ground")]
unique_name_in_owner = true

; The trees, rolled from fixed seeds at _ready() and stood on the lawn that
; wraps three sides of the drive. src/world/grove.gd has the argument for all of
; it — why there are three rows and not six, why the middle of each long one is
; bare, why the end the car drives in from is left open, and why nothing out
; here has a collider.
;
; Its defaults are this driveway's numbers, measured off the model, so the only
; thing overridden is the one thing the scene cannot know on its own: which node
; is the ground. A sibling and not a child of Ground, which is load-bearing
; rather than tidy — Ground.extent() is the merged box of every mesh beneath it
; and five test files read the room's fence off it, so a wood parented in there
; would push that fence out by a crown and move a number nobody meant to move.
[node name="Grove" parent="View/World" instance=ExtResource("7_grove")]
ground = NodePath("../Ground")

; The car, in the middle of the drive, on the floor — and neither of those is a
; number this file can state any more. Every car in the pack is authored about
; its own mid-height (see src/world/mesh_car.gd), and the ten styles are 1.04 m
; to 1.77 m tall, so the one number that used to sit here — 0.7, half the
; blockout's height — now buries a minivan 18 cm into the tarmac or floats a
; sport car the same distance above it. The height is therefore worked out by
; Garage._park_the_car(), which has the argument, out of the car's own box and
; the top of the Ground above; what is written here is that it stands at the
; origin, which is the middle of the drive because the ground was placed to make
; it so.
[node name="Car" parent="View/World" instance=ExtResource("3_car")]
unique_name_in_owner = true

; keep_aspect 0 is KEEP_WIDTH, so the 75 below is the angle measured *across* the
; frame and the angle up and down falls out of the viewport's shape. That is the
; whole reason this node carries a script: at 16:9 it works out at 46.7° down, and
; on a phone held upright — where `window/stretch/aspect = "expand"` grows the
; viewport downward instead of letterboxing — the same 75 works out at 114.8°.
; `Lens` holds a ceiling over that second number by giving up the first. 75 stays
; the lens the shot was framed at, and is what a wide screen still gets.
[node name="Camera" type="Camera3D" parent="View/World"]
unique_name_in_owner = true
script = ExtResource("5_lens")
keep_aspect = 0
current = true
fov = 75.0

[node name="ViewModel" type="Node3D" parent="View/World/Camera"]
unique_name_in_owner = true
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0.17, -0.15, -0.45)
visible = false
script = ExtResource("2_view_model")
